agonal squares, and satisfy the required condition.\n- Since the lower bound $2n-2$ is attained, the minimum number of tiles is $2n-2 = 4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$.  Denote by $U$ the set of uncovered unit squares.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, $U$ consists of precisely $n$ squares, one in each row and one in each column.  Hence $U$ is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation $\\pi$ of $\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$ such that\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,2,\\dots,n\\}.\n\\]\nAll other $n^2-n$ squares must be covered by tiles.  A tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior consists of a set of unit squares; tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor a fixed row $i$ let $\\pi(i)$ be the column of its uncovered square.  The covered squares in row $i$ form at most two contiguous blocks:\n\\[\nL_i=[1,\\pi(i)-1]\\quad\\text{(the \"left\" block, possibly empty)},\n\\qquad\nR_i=[\\pi(i)+1,n]\\quad\\text{(the \"right\" block, possibly empty)}.\n\\]\nIf $\\pi(i)=1$ then $L_i$ is empty and only $R_i$ is present; if $\\pi(i)=n$ then $R_i$ is empty and only $L_i$ is present.  Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, there is precisely one row with $\\pi(i)=1$ and precisely one row with $\\pi(i)=n$.  Consequently the total number of non\u2011empty blocks over all rows is\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{L_i\\neq\\emptyset\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{R_i\\neq\\emptyset\\}\\bigr| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{1}\n\\]\nWe call each such block a *row\u2011interval*.\n\nNow consider any tile $T$.  Let $R(T)$ be the set of rows intersected by $T$ and let $C(T)$ be the set of columns intersected by $T$.  Because $T$ cannot contain an uncovered square, for each $i\\in R(T)$ the whole column set $C(T)$ must lie entirely on one side of the uncovered square $(i,\\pi(i))$.  Hence either\n\\[\nC(T)\\subseteq[1,\\pi(i)-1]\\quad\\text{for all }i\\in R(T)\\quad\\text{(\"left\" tile)}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nC(T)\\subseteq[\\pi(i)+1,n]\\quad\\text{for all }i\\in R(T)\\quad\\text{(\"right\" tile)}.\n\\]\nIn the left case $C(T)$ is of the form $[1,c]$ for some $c$, and the condition $C(T)\\subseteq[1,\\pi(i)-1]$ forces $\\pi(i)>c$ for every $i\\in R(T)$.  In the right case $C(T)=[d,n]$ for some $d$, and we must have $\\pi(i)<d$ for every $i\\in R(T)$.\n\nConsequently a left\u2011hand tile can cover only those row\u2011intervals $L_i$ with $\\pi(i)>c$, and it can cover at most the prefix $[1,c]$ of each such interval.  In particular, let $i_c$ be the unique row with $\\pi(i_c)=c+1$ (such a row exists because $\\pi$ is a permutation).  The left interval of row $i_c$ is exactly $[1,c]$.  The only left\u2011hand tile that can cover any cell of $L_{i_c}$ must have column set $[1,c]$, for a larger column set would contain the uncovered square $(i_c,c+1)$.  Therefore a distinct left\u2011hand tile is required for each $c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,n-1\\}$.\n\nThus at least $n-1$ left\u2011hand tiles are necessary.  An entirely symmetric argument applied to the right intervals $R_i$ shows that at least $n-1$ right\u2011hand tiles are also necessary.  Since a tile cannot be simultaneously left\u2011hand and right\u2011hand, the total number of tiles satisfies\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ \\ge\\ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction using $2n-2$ tiles\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe now exhibit a placement that meets the lower bound.  Choose the permutation $\\pi(i)=i$; i.e., the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells $(i,i)$ for $i=1,2,\\dots,n$.\n\nFor each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n$ place a vertical tile covering the cells\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\nThis tile has size $(j-1)\\times1$ and lies entirely above the diagonal, so it contains no uncovered square.\n\nFor each column $j$ with $1\\le j\\le n-1$ place a vertical tile covering the cells\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThis tile has size $(n-j)\\times1$ and lies entirely below the diagonal, again avoiding all uncovered squares.\n\nThe two families of tiles are disjoint, together they cover every cell of the board except the $n$ diagonal cells, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal one).  The number of tiles used is\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe lower bound (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, and the construction above shows that $2n-2$ tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nTherefore Matilda must place at least $4048$ tiles, and this number can indeed be achieved.\n\n---\n**Answer:** $\\boxed{4048}$\n",
