\\nA=J-P,\\\\]\\nwhere $J$ is the $n\\\\times n$ all‑ones matrix and $P$ is the permutation matrix of $p$ (i.e. $P_{i,p(i)}=1$ and all other entries are $0$).\\nA rectangular tile covering rows $R\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and columns $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ corresponds to the rank‑1 matrix $\\\\mathbf{1}_R\\\\mathbf{1}_C^{\\\\!T}$, and the condition that the tile does not contain an uncovered square is exactly\\n\\\\[\\nC\\\\cap p(R)=\\\\varnothing.\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nThus a tiling of the board is a decomposition of $A$ into a sum of rank‑1 $0$–$1$ matrices satisfying (1) and having pairwise disjoint supports.\\n\\n#### 1.  A lower bound of $n$ tiles\\n\\nThe matrix $J-P$ is invertible.  Indeed, $J$ has eigenvalue $n$ with eigenvector $\\\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\\\dots,1)^{\\\\!T}$ and eigenvalue $0$ on the orthogonal complement.  The permutation matrix $P$ fixes $\\\\mathbf{1}$ (so $P\\\\mathbf{1}=\\\\mathbf{1}$) and its remaining eigenvalues are roots of unity different from $1$.  Consequently the eigenvalues of $J-P$ are\\n\\\\[\\nn-1\\\\quad\\\\text{(once)}\\\\qquad\\\\text{and}\\\\qquad -\\\\lambda\\\\quad\\\\text{for each eigenvalue }\\\\lambda\\\\text{ of }P\\\\text{ on }\\\\mathbf{1}^{\\\\perp}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll these eigenvalues are non‑zero, hence $\\\\det(J-P)\\\\neq0$ and $\\\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n$.\\n\\nEach tile contributes a rank‑1 matrix, therefore if $t$ tiles are used we have\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)\\\\le\\\\sum_{k=1}^t\\\\operatorname{rank}(\\\\mathbf{1}_{R_k}\\\\mathbf{1}_{C_k}^{\\\\!T})=t.\\n\\\\]\\nThus any tiling requires at least $n$ tiles:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{Number of tiles}\\\\ge n}.\\\\]\\n\\n#### 2.  A permutation attaining the bound\\n\\nFor a permutation $p$ define\\n\\\\[\\nL(p)=\\\\#\\\\{i\\\\mid p(i)<\\\\min_{j<i}p(j)\\\\}\\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad R(p)=\\\\#\\\\{i\\\\mid p(i)>\\\\max_{j<i}p(j)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n$L(p)$ counts the left‑to‑right minima of $p$, $R(p)$ the left‑to‑right maxima.