(n-s)=n.\\\\]\\nBy the greedy construction above, the board can be tiled with exactly $n$ rectangles.\\n\\n#### 3.  Verification for $n=2025$\\n\\nTake $n=2025$ and, for concreteness, $s=1013$.  The permutation (2) becomes\\n\\\\[\\np=(1014,1015,\\\\dots,2025,\\\\;1012,1011,\\\\dots,1,\\\\;1013).\\n\\\\]\\nThe uncovered squares are $(i,p(i))$, one in each row and column.  The greedy decomposition yields $L(p)=1013$ left‑hand rectangles and $R(p)=2025-1013=1012$ right‑hand rectangles, altogether $2025$ tiles.  Each tile is a product of a consecutive set of rows and a consecutive set of columns, hence a legitimate rectangular tile, and by construction none of them contains a hole.\\n\\nSince any tiling needs at least $2025$ tiles and we have exhibited a tiling with exactly $2025$ tiles, the minimum possible number of tiles is $2025$.\\n\\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\\n\\n- Verify that the rank argument indeed shows $\\\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n$ for every permutation $P$ (the eigenvalue computation or the kernel argument).  No step should assume $P$ is the identity.\\n- Check the greedy construction: each left‑hand rectangle uses rows $i_{k-1}+1\\\\dots i_k$ and columns $1\\\\dots p(i_k)-1$, where $i_k$ are the indices of left‑to‑right minima.  Confirm that for any row $r$ in this block we have $p(r)\\\\ge p(i_k)$, so the rectangle lies completely left of all holes in those rows.\\n- Do the same for the right‑hand rectangles.\\n- Confirm that the rectangles are disjoint and together cover all squares except the $n$ holes.\\n- Re‑compute $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ for the explicit permutation (2) and confirm $L(p)+R(p)=n$.\\n- Ensure that the final answer field contains only the integer 2025.\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"2025\"\n}"
