  The following greedy geometric decomposition uses exactly $L(p)+R(p)$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Left side (columns $<p(i)$).*  Scan the rows $i=1,\\\\dots,n$.  Whenever $p(i)$ is a new minimum, start a new rectangle covering the rows from the previous such row $+1$ up to $i$ and all columns $1,\\\\dots,p(i)-1$.  Condition (1) holds because for every row in this block the hole lies at a column $\\\\ge p(i)$, hence all columns of the rectangle lie strictly left of the hole.\\n\\n*Right side (columns $>p(i)$).*  Scan the rows again; when $p(i)$ is a new maximum, start a rectangle covering the rows from the previous such row $+1$ up to $i$ and all columns $p(i)+1,\\\\dots,n$.  Again (1) holds because all those columns lie strictly right of each hole in the block.\\n\\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover every square that is not a hole, and their numbers are $L(p)$ and $R(p)$ respectively.  Hence any permutation yields a tiling with $L(p)+R(p)$ tiles.\\n\\nWe now exhibit a permutation for which $L(p)+R(p)=n$.  Choose any integer $s$ with $2\\\\le s\\\\le n-1$ and define\\n\\\\[\\np=(\\\\underbrace{s+1,s+2,\\\\dots,n}_{\\\\text{increasing}},\\\\underbrace{s-1,s-2,\\\\dots,1}_{\\\\text{decreasing}},s).\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nThe first $n-s$ entries are strictly increasing, the next $s-1$ entries are strictly decreasing, and the final entry is $s$.\\n\\n*Computation of $L(p)$.*  The first entry $s+1$ is a new minimum.  In the decreasing block each entry is smaller than all previous ones, so each of the $s-1$ entries $s-1,\\\\dots,1$ is a new minimum.  The final entry $s$ is larger than $1$, hence not a new minimum.  Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nL(p)=1+(s-1)=s.\\\\]\\n\\n*Computation of $R(p)$.*  The increasing block consists of $n-s$ entries, each larger than the previous one, so each of them is a new maximum.  The decreasing block and the final entry never exceed the current maximum $n$, so they contribute nothing.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\nR(p)=n-s.\\\\]\\nThus\\n\\\\[\\nL(p)+R(p)=s+