5$ yields the minimum number $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$ and denote the rows and columns of the $n\\\\times n$ board by $1,2,\\\\dots,n$.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection\\n\\\\[\\n    f:\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\to\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nsuch that the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,f(i))$.\\n\\nDefine the two subsets of covered squares\\n\\\\[\\n    L:=\\\\{(i,j):j<f(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad R:=\\\\{(i,j):j>f(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEvery covered unit square belongs either to $L$ or to $R$, and $L\\\\cap R=\\\\varnothing$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 1 (each side is tiled separately).**\\nAny axis‑aligned rectangle that avoids all holes lies entirely in $L$ or entirely in $R$.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Let $\\\\mathcal{R}=I\\\\times J$ be a rectangle, where $I$ and $J$ are intervals of rows and columns respectively.  For a fixed row $i\\\\in I$, the hole in that row is at column $f(i)$.  If $J$ contains a column $j\\\\ge f(i)$ then the square $(i,f(i))$ would belong to $\\\\mathcal{R}$, contradicting the requirement that holes are uncovered.  Hence for every $i\\\\in I$ either $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,f(i)-1\\\\}$ or $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{f(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  Because $J$ is the same for all rows of $I$, the only possibility is that $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\min_{i\\\\in I}f(i)-1\\\\}$ (the rectangle is left of all holes in $I$) or $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{\\\\max_{i\\\\in I}f(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ (the rectangle is right of all holes in $I$).  In the first case $\\\\mathcal{R}\\\\subseteq L$, in the second $\\\\mathcal{R}\\\\subseteq R$.  ∎\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThus the total number of tiles equals the number of rectangles needed to partition $L$ plus the number needed to partition $R$.  We