quare forces a distinct rectangle, giving $n-1$ rectangles in total.  ∎\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCombining Lemmas&nbsp;2 and&nbsp;3 we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[\\n    \\\\#\\\\text{tiles}\\\\ \\\\ge\\\\ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Construction attaining the bound.**\\nChoose the identity permutation $f(i)=i$ (holes on the main diagonal).  Then\\n\\\\[\\n    L=\\\\{(i,j):j<i\\\\},\\\\qquad R=\\\\{(i,j):j>i\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nFor each $i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ place a horizontal $1\\\\times (n-i)$ rectangle covering the squares $(i,i+1),\\\\dots,(i,n)$.  These $n-1$ rectangles partition $R$.\\n\\nFor each $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ place a vertical $(n-j)\\\\times1$ rectangle covering the squares $(j+1,j),\\\\dots,(n,j)$.  These $n-1$ rectangles partition $L$.\\n\\nAll $n^2-n$ covered squares are tiled exactly once, and the total number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSince the lower bound $2n-2$ is achievable, it is the minimum possible number of tiles.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n    2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the answer is $4048$.\\n\\n### Final Answer ###\\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
