at $L$; the same argument works for $R$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 2 (a lower bound for $L$).**\\nAny partition of $L$ into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least $n-1$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Proof.*  For each $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ let $i_j$ be the unique row with $f(i_j)=j+1$ (such a row exists because $f$ is a permutation).  The square $s_j:=(i_j,j)$ belongs to $L$ and is the rightmost square of row $i_j$ in $L$.\\n\\nConsider any rectangle $\\\\mathcal{R}\\\\subseteq L$ that contains $s_j$.  By Lemma&nbsp;1 the rectangle lies left of all holes in its rows, so its rightmost column $c_{\\\\max}$ satisfies $c_{\\\\max}<f(i_j)=j+1$.  Since $s_j$ itself has column $j$, we must have $c_{\\\\max}=j$; in particular $\\\\mathcal{R}$ cannot contain any square whose column exceeds $j$.\\n\\nNow suppose two distinct indices $j\\\\neq j'$ were covered by the same rectangle $\\\\mathcal{R}$.  Without loss of generality $j<j'$.  Then $\\\\mathcal{R}$ would contain a column $j+1$, because its column interval must be contiguous and contain both $j$ and $j'$.  Row $i_j$ has a hole at column $j+1$, contradicting $\\\\mathcal{R}\\\\subseteq L$.  Hence no rectangle can contain two different squares $s_j$ and $s_{j'}$.\\n\\nThus the $n-1$ distinct squares $s_1,\\\\dots,s_{n-1}$ each require a different rectangle, proving that any partition of $L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  ∎\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe same reasoning applied to the right side yields:\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 3 (a lower bound for $R$).**\\nAny partition of $R$ into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least $n-1$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Proof.*  For each $j\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ let $t_j$ be the unique row with $f(t_j)=j-1$.  The square $(t_j,j)$ is the leftmost square of row $t_j$ in $R$.  By an argument symmetric to Lemma&nbsp;2, each such square forces a distinct rectangle, giving $n-1$ rectangles in total.  ∎\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCombining Lemmas&nbsp;2 and&nbsp;3 we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[\\n    \\\\#\\\\text{tiles}\\\\ \\\\ge\\\\ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Construction attaining the bound.**\\nChoose the identity permutation $f(i)=i$ (holes on the main diagonal).  Then\\n\\\\[\\n    L=\\\\{(i,j):j<i\\\\},\\\\qquad R=\\\\{(i,j):j>i\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nFor each $i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ place a horizontal $1\\\\times (n-i)$ rectangle covering the squares $(i,i+1),\\\\dots,(i,n)$.  These $n-1$ rectangles partition $R$.\\n\\nFor each $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ place a vertical $(n-j)\\\\times1$ rectangle covering the squares $(j+1,j),\\\\dots,(n,j)$.  These $n-1$ rectangles partition $L$.\\n\\nAll $n^2-n$ covered squares are tiled exactly once, and the total number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSince the lower bound $2n-2$ is achievable, it is the minimum possible number of tiles.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n    2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the answer is $4048$.\\n\\n### Final Answer ###\\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
