of unit squares, where \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}\\).  Matilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  Hence there exists a permutation \\(p:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the uncovered squares are\n\\[U=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\\]\nAll other squares must be covered by pairwise disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles).  A tile is a set of the form\n\\[R=A\\times B\\quad\\text{with}\\quad A=\\{a,a+1,\\dots,b\\},\\;B=\\{c,c+1,\\dots,d\\},\\]\nwhere \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  The family of tiles must partition \\([n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor a row \\(i\\) define the *left* and *right* intervals\n\\[L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\\]\nIf \\(p(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\emptyset\\); if \\(p(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i=\\emptyset\\).  In any tiling, a rectangle intersecting row \\(i\\) is either entirely contained in \\(L_i\\) or entirely in \\(R_i\\) (it cannot cross the uncovered cell).  Consequently:\n- If \\(p(i)\\neq1,n\\) then both \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are non\u2011empty, so at least **two** distinct rectangles must intersect row \\(i\\).\n- If \\(p(i)=1\\) or \\(p(i)=n\\) then only one of the intervals is non\u2011empty, so at least **one** rectangle must intersect row \\(i\\).\nSince the permutation uses each column exactly once, there is exactly one row with \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one row with \\(p(i)=n\\).  Hence the total number of row\u2011rectangle incidences satisfies\n\\[\\sum_{R\\in\\mathcal T}\\!\\operatorname{height}(R)\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=1}^n\\!\\#\\{R\\in\\mathcal T\\mid R\\text{ meets row }i\\}\\;\\ge\\;2(n-2)+1+1\\;=\\;2n-2.\\tag{1}\\]\nAn analogous argument applied to columns gives\n\\[\\sum_{R\\in\\mathcal T}\\!\\operatorname{width}(R)\\;\\ge\\;2n-2.\\tag{2}\\]\nEvery rectangle has height at least\u202f1 and width at least\u202f1, therefore each rectangle contributes at least\u202f2 to the sum of the two quantities in (1) and (2).  Consequently\n\\[2\\,|\\mathcal T|\\;\\le\\;\\sum_{R\\in\\mathcal T}\\!\\bigl(\\operatorname{height}(R)+\\operatorname{width}(R)\\bigr)\\;=\\;\\sum_{R}\\!\\operatorname{height}(R)+\\sum_{R}\\!\\operatorname{width}(R)\\;\\ge\\; (2n-2)+(2n-2).\\]\nDividing by\u202f2 we obtain the universal lower bound\n\\[|\\mathcal T|\\;\\ge\\;2n-2.\\tag{3}\\]\nThus any tiling that satisfies the condition must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nTake the *identity* permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\\[V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\\]\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the horizontal rectangle\n\\[H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\\]\nThe sets \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^n\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=2}^n\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all diagonal squares, and together they cover every off\u2011diagonal square because:\n- If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).\n- If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).\nThus we obtain a tiling with exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\]\n\n### Final Answer ###\nThe minimum number of tiles Matilda needs is \\boxed{4048}.",
